<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <BuiltInComInteropSupport>true</BuiltInComInteropSupport>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>true</AvaloniaUseCompiledBindingsByDefault>
    <RootNamespace>Yoink</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Yoink</AssemblyName>

    <!--
      Footprint. release.yml publishes self-contained (bundles the whole .NET runtime, since
      Velopack's self-updating package model needs that — see UpdateService's doc comment), which
      makes runtime-level size/memory knobs actually matter for what ships, not just dev-loop
      builds. Every number below is a real `du -sh` on an actual `dotnet publish -c Release -r
      linux-x64 self-contained true` output, not an estimate — checked one setting at a time
      rather than assumed, same as this file's other "confirmed, not assumed" notes.

      InvariantGlobalization made no measurable difference to the on-disk publish size by itself
      here (110M before and after) — this SDK's linux-x64 self-contained publish doesn't bundle a
      separate ICU data file to begin with (unlike some Windows self-contained builds, which do).
      It's kept anyway for what it still genuinely buys: skipping ICU initialization/loading at
      process startup, which is a real (if modest) runtime-memory and startup-time saving that
      doesn't show up in a publish-folder size diff. Confirmed safe to flip on: grepped the whole
      codebase for ToUpper/ToLower/string.Compare/CompareTo/TextInfo — none exist. Every
      DateTimeOffset/double already goes through CultureInfo.InvariantCulture explicitly
      (DownloadQueueService), and the one bare int.Parse (AddDownloadDialog's resolution combo)
      only ever sees plain ASCII digits. If a future feature adds real locale-aware text handling,
      revisit this rather than assuming it's still safe.

      Workstation GC (ServerGarbageCollection=false) with background collection
      (ConcurrentGarbageCollection=true) are WinExe's own defaults already — set explicitly here so
      that stays true on purpose rather than by accident of project type. Server GC trades memory
      for throughput (one heap per core), which is the wrong trade for a single-window desktop app
      that's mostly idle: Workstation GC's one small heap plus background collection (so a gen2
      collection doesn't stall the UI thread) is the low-memory, low-pause combination this app
      actually wants.

      PublishTrimmed is where the real on-disk win is: 110M → 52M (53% smaller) on the same
      linux-x64 self-contained publish. Needed AppSettingsJsonContext to exist first —
      SettingsService's old reflection-based JsonSerializer.Serialize/Deserialize<AppSettings>
      calls are exactly what the trimmer can't statically prove are safe, and a trimmed publish
      before that fix emitted IL2026 warnings pointing at those two calls specifically (i.e. this
      wasn't hypothetical risk — the trimmer caught a real one, right where reflection-based JSON
      always is the risk). After that fix, a trimmed publish emits zero warnings from this
      codebase; the one remaining warning (IL2104, Avalonia.Controls.DataGrid) is a transitive
      FluentAvaloniaUI dependency Yoink's own code never references (grepped — no `DataGrid`
      anywhere in Yoink/), so trimming can't have broken something nothing calls. Also re-ran the
      full `dotnet test` suite (107/107) and the headless-render MainWindow check (see the
      headless-visual-verification project memory) against the change. What that verification
      does NOT cover: actually launching the packaged, trimmed, self-contained executable on a
      real windowed desktop — this dev sandbox has no display and no way to simulate one (same
      constraint noted in that memory). Treat the first trimmed release the same way release.yml
      already treats Windows/macOS: worth an explicit real-hardware smoke test before calling it
      fully verified, rather than assuming clean warnings + passing tests is the complete picture.
    -->
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>false</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <PublishTrimmed>true</PublishTrimmed>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AvaloniaResource Include="Assets/**" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Lets Yoink.Tests reach the handful of pure-logic methods (schedule/rate-limit math, yt-dlp
         JSON parsing, etc.) that are internal rather than public purely for that reason — see each
         one's own doc comment. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Yoink.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentAvaloniaUI" Version="3.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.Sqlite" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Velopack" Version="1.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
